00:06:24.464 --> 00:06:25.130
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:25.630
Cool.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.780
So we said "abuse" a
few times through that.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.015
What does cloud
abuse actually mean?

00:06:32.015 --> 00:06:33.537
Emeka, why don't you go first?

00:06:33.537 --> 00:06:34.370
EMEKA OKONKWO: Sure.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.860
So cloud abuse, the
way it's defined,

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:40.140
is any kind of
activity that ends up

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.470
violating the cloud terms of
services or acceptable user

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:44.270
policy.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.520
So our team's goal,
or mission, is

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:49.720
to basically enforce the
relevant sections of the cloud

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:52.481
terms of services/AUP.

00:06:52.481 --> 00:06:53.980
MARK MANDEL: Could
you give a couple

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.355
examples of what kind
of terms of services

00:06:56.355 --> 00:06:57.527
that you normally handle?

00:06:57.527 --> 00:06:58.360
EMEKA OKONKWO: Sure.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.280
So things such as,
like, denial of terms

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.560
attacks that emanate from the
cloud platform, or intrusion

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:06.720
attacks, or even
malware or phishing.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.710
Those kind of activities
are the things

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:11.797
that we are responsible for
protecting or preventing.

00:07:11.797 --> 00:07:13.380
SWATI KULSHRESHTH:
And to add to that,

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:15.520
the things that would
violate the terms of service,

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:17.600
like you're going beyond
your quota restrictions

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.433
or doing things that
you shouldn't be doing,

00:07:19.433 --> 00:07:22.646
given the cloud services
that you're using.

00:07:22.646 --> 00:07:23.640
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.000
And so you're saying that
basically, what you're doing

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.360
is not protecting Google
Cloud Platform customers

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:34.290
from external abuse, but
actually stopping abuse coming

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:35.820
from Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.528
SWATI KULSHRESHTH:
That is exactly right.

00:07:37.528 --> 00:07:40.330
Our team is focused on looking
at abuse that originated

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.260
from the Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.910
And that is the kind of
activity that we specifically

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:46.680
look for and try to stop.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.430
EMEKA OKONKWO: Attacks
that come to Google

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:50.820
is handled by Site
Reliability Engineers.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.300
So that's a different team.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:52.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:54.060
We've talked with
a couple of them.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:55.980
We should keep on talking
with more of them.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.570
They're always so
cool to talk to.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:07:58.236
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:07:58.236 --> 00:08:01.930
So clearly, the people who
listen to our podcasts,

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:03.055
they're responsible people.

00:08:03.055 --> 00:08:04.096
They're wonderful people.

00:08:04.096 --> 00:08:05.005
We love them dearly.

00:08:05.005 --> 00:08:06.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
They're listeners.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:07.090
MARK MANDEL: They're listeners.

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:08.320
So clearly, they're great.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.130
They're not going to actively
go out and try and do

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.070
things that are
malicious or nefarious

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:14.540
or something like that.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:18.080
Why should they care
about Cloud Abuse?

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.900
Is there issues in
terms of resources?

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.090
What sort of problems is
this solving for people

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:24.807
who are customers of GCP?

00:08:24.807 --> 00:08:26.640
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: So
there are two of abuse

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.427
we see coming from Google Cloud.

00:08:28.427 --> 00:08:29.510
There's intentional abuse.

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:32.176
And clearly, like you said, your
listeners are not one of those.

00:08:32.176 --> 00:08:34.720
But then there is the
unintentional abuse.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.260
You did not want to be
conducting denial of service

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:39.850
attempts or intrusion attempts.

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:42.520
But your system has
been hacked or there's

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:44.250
software that you
downloaded, and that

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:45.960
is doing it on your behalf.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.972
And those are the kinds of
things that we are looking for.

00:08:48.972 --> 00:08:50.680
And those are the kind
of things that you

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.079
should be looking at too.

00:08:52.079 --> 00:08:53.620
EMEKA OKONKWO: And
to add on to that,

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.450
it could be possible
that, for example,

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:57.749
if someone hacks your
system-- so again,

00:08:57.749 --> 00:08:59.040
that's a breach on your system.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.302
You really don't want to
be responsible for that.

00:09:01.302 --> 00:09:03.510
Or at least, you want to
find out as soon as possible

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:04.570
if that happens.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:07.140
Or if someone is
using your machine

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.290
to perform a denial
of terms attack,

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:12.374
that could cost a lot
of money on the cloud,

00:09:12.374 --> 00:09:14.040
so a lot of computing
resources that you

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.030
would have to be
responsible for paying for.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:18.230
So hopefully, if we
catch that in time,

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:19.820
that will be very
awesome for you.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.470
And also, finally, if you
have an external website that

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:25.560
is hosted somewhere,
we are trying

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.340
to protect you from being hit
by people who are on cloud.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:31.264
So makes life better
for everybody.

00:09:31.264 --> 00:09:32.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:35.330
I'm curious-- you mentioned
denial of service attacks.

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:38.820
What other kinds of use
cases do you normally see?

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:43.480
Is it people getting
infected with some bots?

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.224
What kind of problem
that you normally handle?

00:09:46.224 --> 00:09:48.640
EMEKA OKONKWO: So we see a lot
of denial of terms attacks.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:51.090
We also see a lot of
brute forcing activities.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.620
So basically, once
a machine maybe

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.620
is hacked-- because of
course, none of our users

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:56.500
are going to do it.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.160
So a compromised
machine could be

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.700
trying to probe vulnerabilities
against external networks.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:06.600
So trying to maybe log
into those networks.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.740
Or even vulnerability
scanning to see

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:12.200
what's easy to be falling prey.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:14.930
One of the things we
saw recently some time

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:19.840
back was when WordPress had some
vulnerabilities [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:23.550
We saw a lot of attacks from
Google targeting third party

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:28.740
WordPress sites,
or even other cloud

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:30.130
customers who had WordPress.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:32.050
Those kind of
things are examples.

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:34.008
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: I
think one of the things we

00:10:34.008 --> 00:10:37.080
see very frequently is that
your firewalls have not

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:38.039
been set up properly.

00:10:38.039 --> 00:10:40.080
And then somebody manages
to get into your system

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:42.240
and start doing either
denial of service attacks,

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.650
or brute forcing, or any of the
other suspicious activities.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:48.234
MARK MANDEL: I guess
that means if I'm

00:10:48.234 --> 00:10:50.025
running my service on
Google Cloud Platform

00:10:50.025 --> 00:10:51.810
and someone's hacked
me, then suddenly, I

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.820
don't have those resources on
that machine available to me

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.890
anymore if someone's
trying to mine bitcoins

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:59.980
or whatever it is the cool
kids are doing these days.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:01.170
Suddenly, my CPU--

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:02.378
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Those coins.

00:11:02.378 --> 00:11:03.790
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, exactly.

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:05.160
My CPU is suddenly at 100%.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:06.970
And so I can't serve
traffic or do anything

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:08.137
I should be doing on my app.

00:11:08.137 --> 00:11:09.011
EMEKA OKONKWO: Right.

00:11:09.011 --> 00:11:10.886
And even, like you said,
it could be possible

00:11:10.886 --> 00:11:12.719
that you could have
legitimate users who are

00:11:12.719 --> 00:11:13.950
using the platform properly.

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:15.760
But because the
resources are being

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:18.390
used by these
irresponsible people,

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.220
or by people who have
been compromised,

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:22.120
they don't have the
ability to actually enjoy

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:23.135
what they paid for.

00:11:23.135 --> 00:11:25.830
So that was a bad experience
for other customers

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:27.227
who are not doing abuse.

00:11:27.227 --> 00:11:28.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
most of the things

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:31.570
that we've discussed
so far are basically

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:35.280
people attacking or
somehow compromising

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.340
the account of someone
that is well-intentioned.

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:40.690
Does it also happen that
someone is well-intentioned

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:43.820
but somehow, they are not
aware of some limitations?

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.051
What kind of limitations
people sometimes forget?

00:11:46.051 --> 00:11:47.800
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: I
think the biggest one

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:50.660
that we see in this
category is people

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:54.320
going outside of the
quota available to them

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:56.230
and using it multiple times.

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:58.810
So associated with
the free trial,

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:00.490
there are specific
quota limitations

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:02.950
in terms of number of projects,
in terms of how many emails

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:05.990
you can send, how much
network you can use.

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:11.350
And that is the one that people
often end up circumventing

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:14.420
and then getting caught
by one of the rules.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:17.040
We do try to give a lot
of benefit of doubt.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:19.550
So unless you're really
egregiously violating that,

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:22.080
we'll probably not
come after you.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:22.920
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:24.461
EMEKA OKONKWO: And
also, for example,

00:12:24.461 --> 00:12:27.510
what we see as well, in addition
to what Swati had mentioned,

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:30.330
is that a lot of people
may not necessarily

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:34.170
take the precautions of
securing their instances

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:37.170
or set up the appropriate
firewalls to only allow

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:41.700
the traffic from the
designated VMs or instances.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.830
So what happens is that
someone may basically

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:48.420
hack into their systems and
use their systems as a part

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:50.015
of a bot to do something bad.

00:12:50.015 --> 00:12:53.890
So not taking the extra
couple of minutes required

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:55.510
to actually lock
down your machine

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:58.250
to make sure that you only get
traffic to and fro from what

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:01.290
you really want to has
been a lot of concern.

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:03.040
MARK MANDEL: You were
talking a little bit

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:05.685
as well just about
accidental stuff people do.

00:13:05.685 --> 00:13:07.310
You talked a little
bit about projects.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:08.890
So are there any
other sort of things

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:11.700
that people often go,
oh, it'll be fine,

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.460
or they don't even
realize that that

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:15.450
was a terms of service
thing that they go past,

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:16.782
that people should be aware of?

00:13:16.782 --> 00:13:18.490
SWATI KULSHRESHTH:
The most frequent ones

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:26.432
is the quotas and the number
of projects and email quotas.

00:13:26.432 --> 00:13:28.390
EMEKA OKONKWO: And also,
sometimes, some people

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:31.060
may use the platform
as a hosting provider

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:35.080
so that, basically, other
people can run off of them

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.850
as part of the services they
offer to their own customers.

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:40.280
So if they don't properly
monitor those customers

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:43.360
or check the traffic,
they could also

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.570
end up getting a violations
of our terms of services.

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:49.040
So for example, if they open
up their service to a host,

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:53.030
like upload images, some
of them may be malware,

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:55.540
which is now being served
from the cloud customer

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:56.950
to external people.

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:58.576
So again, that's
also a violation.

00:13:58.576 --> 00:14:00.610
So it may be not
you specifically.

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:03.000
But the people that
you provide services to

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.320
could end up costing
you a violation.

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:06.820
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that makes sense.

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:09.960
Anywhere you've got a system
that has user-generated content

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.120
or anything like that, you
need to be very careful

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:14.447
about what content
they're putting up there

00:14:14.447 --> 00:14:16.780
and what it's possible to
enable to do on your platform.

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:17.770
EMEKA OKONKWO: That's correct.

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:18.686
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:18.686 --> 00:14:21.914
So let's imagine that at
some point, for some reason,

00:14:21.914 --> 00:14:24.080
either because by mistake,
they didn't pay attention

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.920
to terms of service, or somebody
got hacked, I get an email.

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:32.860
And the email is
basically saying, hey,

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:36.770
we detected some fraudulent
activity in your account.

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:37.360
What's next?

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:38.970
What do I have to do?

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.420
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: So
I can take that one.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:42.930
When we see
something suspicious,

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.455
we will send out an
email to you saying,

00:14:45.455 --> 00:14:47.650
we're seeing this
suspicious activity.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:48.330
Please check it.

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:49.270
Please get back to us.

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:50.000
Send an appeal.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:51.914
And there will be a
three day warning period.

00:14:51.914 --> 00:14:53.330
The thing you need
to keep in mind

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:56.520
is that this email goes
out to the project owners.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:00.295
So definitely monitor
your project owner email.

00:15:00.295 --> 00:15:03.340
We hear a lot of developers
coming back to us saying, hey,

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:04.470
I wasn't monitoring it.

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.280
So I didn't realize that
there's something going on.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:09.550
MARK MANDEL: I've heard
stories about that.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:11.010
I think that's a great takeaway.

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:12.260
I just want to highlight that.

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:14.218
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
happened to us, actually,

00:15:14.218 --> 00:15:19.230
where we got an email from
our internal email, the Google

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:23.900
Glass Platform podcast, about
some certificate going away.

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:25.572
And we're like, whoops.

00:15:25.572 --> 00:15:28.187
MARK MANDEL: We
somehow new about that.

00:15:28.187 --> 00:15:29.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:30.410
That's a very important point.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:31.872
Pay attention to the emails.

00:15:31.872 --> 00:15:33.830
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: So
the process going forward

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:35.910
is you go to the
Developer console.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:37.570
It will take you to
the Appeals page.

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:39.580
And you send an appeal.

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:41.370
The good thing to
do about the appeal

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:45.234
is to actually give us some
context around what you're

00:15:45.234 --> 00:15:46.650
doing, what your
project is doing,

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:49.830
and why do you think that
suspicious activity might

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:50.627
be happening.

00:15:50.627 --> 00:15:52.710
We definitely don't need
the code of your program.

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.310
We don't need a design diagram.

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:57.217
But we do need some context
to see why this was happening.

00:15:57.217 --> 00:15:59.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How
much information do you

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:00.280
give out of this?

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:03.280
Will be something like, you're
going over the quota on this,

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.650
or you're using too much
CPU, or something like that?

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:07.100
Or it's just like, hey.

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:09.494
What kind of
information do I get?

00:16:09.494 --> 00:16:11.160
SWATI KULSHRESHTH:
We definitely provide

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:13.170
information on what kind
of suspicious activity

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:13.753
we are seeing.

00:16:13.753 --> 00:16:15.672
So we will tell
you, we are seeing

00:16:15.672 --> 00:16:17.130
denial of service
attacks, or we're

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:19.860
seeing you circumventing quota.

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:23.430
We will give you the project ID,
the project name, and the time

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:25.270
on which that happened.

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:27.270
If you need more information,
you can definitely

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:28.060
write back to us.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:29.476
And sometimes we
can help you out.

00:16:29.476 --> 00:16:32.320
Sometimes, you need to go back
and look at your own traffic

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:34.610
logs and see what was
happening around that time.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:36.110
MARK MANDEL: So it
sounds like I get

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:39.265
the email, which I'm monitoring
because I look at my emails.

00:16:39.265 --> 00:16:40.640
And you give me
some information.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.240
So it really sounds like
the first step is actually

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.326
for me to go and have
a look at my project

00:16:44.326 --> 00:16:46.200
and see if I can work
out what's going wrong.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:47.750
And maybe I need to lock
something down better,

00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:49.885
or just stop doing something
that I probably shouldn't have

00:16:49.885 --> 00:16:50.816
been doing in the first place.

00:16:50.816 --> 00:16:51.800
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: That's right.

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:54.250
EMEKA OKONKWO: And also, we
surface the same notifications

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:55.740
in the Developer console.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:58.000
So you can log into
your Developer console

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:59.300
and see the same information.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.130
So at least you will
see a notification

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:03.509
next to your project
with some icon, something

00:17:03.509 --> 00:17:04.550
which you can mouse over.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:08.920
It gives you more information
about high level, hey,

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:09.930
we saw something wrong.

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:10.760
Can you please take a look?

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:11.880
It needs your attention.

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:13.720
So that's another
way that you also

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:15.720
get the information about
something going wrong.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:17.553
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: To
add to what Emeka just

00:17:17.553 --> 00:17:19.380
said, if you are on
the Developer console,

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:21.420
go look at the Dashboard
for your projects.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:22.869
And you will definitely see.

00:17:22.869 --> 00:17:24.450
If there's an
issue, you will see

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:26.660
that surfaced on the Dashboard.

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:27.910
MARK MANDEL: Now, I'm curious.

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:30.580
If I go looking-- maybe
I'm not that technical.

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:33.450
Or for whatever reason, I can't
work out what the issue is.

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:35.030
Can I come to you
and say, hey, look.

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:35.580
I'm stuck.

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:37.590
Can you give me some
extra information?

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:38.839
Is that a thing you will do?

00:17:38.839 --> 00:17:39.630
EMEKA OKONKWO: Yes.

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:41.950
You can come and ask us
for more information.

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:44.610
There are certain things that
we can share or not share.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:46.920
So to the extent
that we can, we will

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:49.180
provide as much information
as we can to help you.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:51.860
But if you're not really sure
what's going on with your VM,

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:54.450
for example, normally, we would
recommend that you kind of do

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:56.840
a re-install just to make
sure that you wipe out

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:00.120
any person who may have
hacked into your machine.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:04.030
MARK MANDEL: Provision your
machines with a script.

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:05.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That makes sense.

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:08.844
So could you share a little
bit out of your experience.

00:18:08.844 --> 00:18:10.760
You've been working on
this for quite a while.

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:14.100
What is some cool anecdotes
of people that have

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.384
gone through the abuse process?

00:18:16.384 --> 00:18:18.300
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: I
think the most common one

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:19.600
is I'm not doing anything.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:22.240
I didn't do anything wrong.

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:23.019
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:23.019 --> 00:18:25.060
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: And
then when we talk to them,

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:26.880
and they'll go back,
look at the traffic logs.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:27.490
And they'll say, oh yeah.

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:28.374
I see this activity.

00:18:28.374 --> 00:18:29.290
It must have happened.

00:18:29.290 --> 00:18:31.525
Because I'm using a
newly installed software,

00:18:31.525 --> 00:18:33.680
or this application
must be doing it.

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:36.190
There are times when some people
have installed apps and not

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:38.106
even realized that those
apps are doing things

00:18:38.106 --> 00:18:41.320
on their behalf, which is
getting them to violate

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:42.930
the terms of conditions.

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:46.680
And when you get
that email saying

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:49.610
you're violating the terms
of service or something

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:51.770
is wrong or suspicious is
happening, go check it.

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:52.970
You may not be doing it.

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.382
Someone else may be doing
it from your system.

00:18:55.382 --> 00:18:57.340
EMEKA OKONKWO: Even to
add on to what she said,

00:18:57.340 --> 00:18:59.420
sometimes we've let
people know, hey,

00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:03.600
we detected xyz is
happening on your platform.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.617
And then they come back
to us and say, oh no.

00:19:05.617 --> 00:19:06.700
Nothing is going on wrong.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:07.830
Everything is fine.

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:11.190
And then later on, we get
a report from a third party

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:14.170
also saying, hey, we got abusive
traffic from this person.

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:15.020
And so when we now
send it to them,

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:16.160
they're like, oh my goodness.

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:17.076
So something is wrong.

00:19:17.076 --> 00:19:18.310
What's going on?

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:20.940
So usually when we do
say something is wrong,

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:22.400
try to take a look.

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:26.344
Because more likely than not,
there is something going on.

00:19:26.344 --> 00:19:27.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And if you're not

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:29.501
able to find it, just
reply saying, hey,

00:19:29.501 --> 00:19:30.500
I'm not able to find it.

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:31.125
Give me a hand.

00:19:31.125 --> 00:19:32.060
EMEKA OKONKWO: Right.

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:33.130
MARK MANDEL: You made
an interesting point

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:33.800
there very quickly.

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:35.310
You work with third
parties as well

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:39.059
who also report, basically,
that traffic or something bad is

00:19:39.059 --> 00:19:40.350
happening in particular places?

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:40.660
EMEKA OKONKWO: Yes.

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:41.510
That's correct.

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:43.640
So we also receive
reports from third parties

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:46.970
who are reporting attacks
that they receive coming

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:48.940
from the Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:51.220
So it's a part of our
duties, in addition

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:53.740
to protecting cloud customers,
is to also take that

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:55.260
into consideration.

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.030
Basically, if you
verify the report,

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:59.360
because not everybody
who tells you is-- maybe

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:00.836
they have some spiteful stuff.

00:20:00.836 --> 00:20:02.460
But you have to verify
that it actually

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:03.670
is a legitimate report.

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.664
And if so, we also
act on those as well.

00:20:05.664 --> 00:20:06.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:09.710
So what about when
people are hosting

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:12.000
content that is fraudulent?

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:14.910
So it's not really about
what you are doing,

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:16.720
but about what you're serving.

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:20.150
So for instance, let's
imagine some pirated movie

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:22.320
or stuff like that.

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:25.240
EMEKA OKONKWO: Well,
we all love movies.

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:26.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, we do.

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:28.650
EMEKA OKONKWO: So
if someone is found

00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:32.540
to be hosting some sort of movie
that is copyright protected,

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:35.630
or falls under the
DMCA act, what we

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:38.940
do when we receive such
reports is that we actually

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:40.850
work with our
legal team and send

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:42.360
those notifications to them.

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:44.440
Because that is not
necessarily abuse,

00:20:44.440 --> 00:20:46.960
but it might be something that
falls under legal category.

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:51.550
So basically, our role in
that is just mostly forward

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:54.100
those to the legal team so that
they can move it from there.

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:55.850
MARK MANDEL: But that
would be a violation

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:56.740
of the terms of service.

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:57.380
EMEKA OKONKWO: It
would be a violation

00:20:57.380 --> 00:20:58.200
of the terms of service.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:20:58.670
Yes.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:20:59.540
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:01.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And at some point,

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:03.476
you mentioned that very often,
it's about vulnerabilities.

00:21:03.476 --> 00:21:08.730
And sometimes it's applications
that are malicious, really.

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:10.770
But what about when
there is something

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:12.670
that all of a sudden
everybody knows about,

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:15.450
and all the internet
is unsafe again,

00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:19.079
like Heartbleed, for instance.

00:21:19.079 --> 00:21:20.870
EMEKA OKONKWO: In those
kind of situations,

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:23.785
when this had happened, we work
closely with the Cloud Security

00:21:23.785 --> 00:21:26.610
Team to come up with
some solutions that

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:28.340
can help to protect
our customers

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:32.170
and like use that or deploy
that to help to protect them.

00:21:32.170 --> 00:21:35.181
So we've had some situations,
like the Heartbleed one.

00:21:35.181 --> 00:21:37.180
One that comes to mind
is one called Raw Hammer,

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:39.470
which was a big security issue.

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:41.720
And we worked with the
Cloud Security Team

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:44.530
to help us to come up with
appropriate mitigation

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:46.530
steps that can be applied
on the Cloud Platform.

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:48.404
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Can
you explain a little bit

00:21:48.404 --> 00:21:49.310
what Raw Hammer is?

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:51.170
I've never heard about it.

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:52.795
EMEKA OKONKWO: I
don't know what it is.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:54.800
MARK MANDEL: We'll put a
link in the show notes.

00:21:54.800 --> 00:21:55.390
We'll do some research.

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:55.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:56.280
We'll find it.

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:57.650
That's an interesting one.

00:21:57.650 --> 00:21:58.864
Cool.

00:21:58.864 --> 00:22:00.030
MARK MANDEL: So that's cool.

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:00.530
All right.

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:03.751
Well, I think we should talk
about DocGraph, I think.

00:22:03.751 --> 00:22:05.000
It's the elephant in the room.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:06.950
We should probably
talk about it.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:10.184
If anyone hasn't heard about
it, it was [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:10.184 --> 00:22:11.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:12.200
Very interesting story.

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:15.720
Basically, it went
crazy on hacker news.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:21.300
Someone said, oh yeah, Google
Cloud basically shut down

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:22.880
my company completely.

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:25.860
And I think it's interesting
because it was shut down

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.960
by Cloud Abuse, your team.

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:30.457
So let's talk about that.

00:22:30.457 --> 00:22:31.040
What happened?

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:33.740
Could you tell us a little
bit about the internals, what

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:35.360
did actually happen in there?

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:36.193
EMEKA OKONKWO: Yeah.

00:22:36.193 --> 00:22:38.210
So what happened was
that we had actually

00:22:38.210 --> 00:22:41.150
received some third party
reports about attacks

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:43.294
coming from the user's machine.

00:22:43.294 --> 00:22:46.060
And after we independently
verified the situation,

00:22:46.060 --> 00:22:48.450
we sent the user a
notification, which

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:50.110
comes with a grace period.

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:52.620
And so the user did
receive the notification.

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:56.190
But it seems that there were
some problems on the Developer

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:58.520
console which prevented
the user from being

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:01.800
able to submit a successful
appeal within the time period

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:02.720
that he had.

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:07.060
And so unfortunately, after
the time period had expired,

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:09.480
he actually had his
project suspended.

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:11.400
And so that's when
he wrote the email.

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:13.824
MARK MANDEL: Now, it sounds
like there was some reach out

00:23:13.824 --> 00:23:15.990
from the Google Cloud Team,
and the Cloud Abuse Team

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:17.620
worked with him.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:20.360
So we ended up getting
the project reinstated?

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:21.610
EMEKA OKONKWO: That's correct.

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:22.150
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: Yes.

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:23.650
MARK MANDEL: And
we've been working,

00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:26.674
I believe, with him as
well to add to improvements

00:23:26.674 --> 00:23:28.840
and basically improve things
that went wrong before.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:30.330
Can you talk a little
about those as well?

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:30.640
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: Yes.

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:33.080
So that issue actually
highlighted a few things

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:35.360
that had been happening
or that were not great

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:37.810
as far as the developer
experience was concerned.

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:41.380
And when this issue
highlighted those,

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:43.582
we've been working
hard to fix them.

00:23:43.582 --> 00:23:45.040
We've made a few
changes to the way

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:47.600
we inform developers on the
email, as well as the Developer

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:49.700
console, so that they
have more information

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:52.897
to take corrective steps.

00:23:55.580 --> 00:23:57.640
Our team worked really
hard to fix those issues.

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:01.215
And those were fixed within a
week of this issue happening.

00:24:01.215 --> 00:24:02.090
EMEKA OKONKWO: Right.

00:24:02.090 --> 00:24:06.060
So basically, it was the
detection was correct.

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:07.840
The issue was correct.

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:10.100
But we didn't have a
good enough experience

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:11.800
from a developer's
standpoint for them

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:13.745
to communicate and
reach out to us.

00:24:13.745 --> 00:24:14.870
That doesn't always happen.

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:15.910
It was a unique set
of circumstances

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:17.160
that happened for him.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:19.920
Because we do get people who
are able to successfully appeal.

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:22.970
But kind of like when
something goes wrong,

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:25.210
everything goes wrong
kind of situation.

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:27.540
So that's actually what
happened in his situation.

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:28.998
MARK MANDEL: I'm
super happy to say

00:24:28.998 --> 00:24:33.150
that he has said, "I'm very
pleased with the service

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:35.980
overall and will likely remain
a customer for a long time."

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:38.450
So very happy to hear that
the resolution has made

00:24:38.450 --> 00:24:40.050
a deep and meaningful impact.

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:41.723
So super happy about that.

00:24:41.723 --> 00:24:42.530
Cool.

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:43.905
Well, in case we
missed anything,

00:24:43.905 --> 00:24:45.404
I need to ask our
standard question.

00:24:45.404 --> 00:24:48.010
Is there anything else you want
to talk about or highlight,

00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:49.020
or maybe new things?

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:51.049
Or anything we
might have missed?

00:24:51.049 --> 00:24:53.340
EMEKA OKONKWO: We have a
couple of Help Center articles

00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:55.100
that basically give
you some explanations

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:57.850
about some of the policy
violations and also steps

00:24:57.850 --> 00:24:59.680
of how you can
secure your instance.

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:02.350
So as we see a lot of times,
people are compromised.

00:25:02.350 --> 00:25:04.050
So if you can take
a look at those,

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:05.867
that will really help you out.

00:25:05.867 --> 00:25:07.450
Prevention is better
than cure, right?

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:08.370
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:25:08.370 --> 00:25:10.244
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: Our
emails will direct you

00:25:10.244 --> 00:25:11.080
to those articles.

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:13.310
So if it is about
just figuring out

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:15.152
how to get through
this process, we

00:25:15.152 --> 00:25:16.610
have a Help Center
article on that.

00:25:16.610 --> 00:25:19.480
We have Help Center articles
on specific abuse types

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:23.327
that we will be sending
you emails about.

00:25:23.327 --> 00:25:25.410
We're also trying to
proactively reach out to you.

00:25:25.410 --> 00:25:27.743
Because one of the things
that we've been talking about,

00:25:27.743 --> 00:25:30.949
a common theme, is the
instance has been compromised.

00:25:30.949 --> 00:25:33.490
So we're trying to reach out to
developers whose instances we

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:35.760
see could be at a high
risk of being compromised

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.890
before the compromise happens.

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.194
So keep watching out
for those emails.

00:25:40.194 --> 00:25:41.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:43.140
We'll have the links
to those articles

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:44.680
directly from the
show notes here.

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:48.520
So I would say that then, one
of the biggest pieces of advice

00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:50.988
is pay attention to your emails.

00:25:50.988 --> 00:25:51.946
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: Yes.

00:25:51.946 --> 00:25:53.510
EMEKA OKONKWO: Or
to the dashboard.

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:54.926
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
To the dashboard.

00:25:54.926 --> 00:25:57.700
What could be the second biggest
piece of advice for someone

00:25:57.700 --> 00:26:00.120
that wants to make
sure that they're

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:04.320
going to get impacted by fraud?

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:07.050
EMEKA OKONKWO: I would say make
sure your instances are secure.

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:08.370
Check your firewalls.

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:12.310
A lot of times, everything
is open to everybody.

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:14.220
So it may be easier
to get started

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:16.150
faster, which is awesome.

00:26:16.150 --> 00:26:18.484
But do take the extra time
to actually lock things down.

00:26:18.484 --> 00:26:19.858
SWATI KULSHRESHTH:
I think to add

00:26:19.858 --> 00:26:22.970
to that, the third one is a lot
of times, the software you've

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:25.840
installed on your
instance, just make

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:27.650
sure all the security
patches are in place

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:28.940
and keep on top of that.

00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:30.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So basically, just

00:26:30.590 --> 00:26:33.150
make sure no one else gets
to use your resources.

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:34.150
SWATI KULSHRESHTH: Yeah.

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:34.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:26:34.610 --> 00:26:36.480
Well, thank you so much
for taking the time

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:37.740
to talk to us about
abuse yesterday.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:38.500
EMEKA OKONKWO: You're welcome.

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:39.920
Thank you so much
for inviting us.

00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:40.878
MARK MANDEL: Thank you.

00:26:40.878 --> 00:26:43.550
Thanks for joining us
today, Swati and Emeka.

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:44.850
Super interesting discussion.

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:47.560
And I definitely learned a
lot about the abuse process,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.544
and why it exists, and why I
think it's really so important.

00:26:50.544 --> 00:26:51.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:53.390
I think that the
most important thing

00:26:53.390 --> 00:26:59.940
was Abuse is here only because
we need to protect you too,

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:01.140
which is really interesting.

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:02.900
And how most of the
time, when Abuse

00:27:02.900 --> 00:27:05.430
is dealing with customers,
it's because they

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:06.760
might have been compromised.

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:09.170
So take care.

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:13.920
Make sure that your network
is correctly configured

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:19.030
and you have all your networking
firewall rules and all

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.430
that stuff set correctly.

00:27:21.430 --> 00:27:23.135
Which, by the way,
that's what we will

00:27:23.135 --> 00:27:25.090
be talking about next episode.

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:28.150
So maybe that's
what we should do.

00:27:28.150 --> 00:27:29.850
Listen to the next
episode next week.

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:33.600
But I think now,
it's time to go talk

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:35.170
about the question of the week.

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:37.350
And the question of
the week is something

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:40.790
that I really like because
I'm a pretty lazy person.

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:44.955
And sometimes I see very
often examples of this

00:27:44.955 --> 00:27:47.430
is how you run
Jenkins on Kubernetes,

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:54.150
or this is how you run Drupal
or WordPress on Kubernetes,

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:55.320
whatever.

00:27:55.320 --> 00:27:59.220
And it really make me feel,
well, on Google Cloud Platform,

00:27:59.220 --> 00:28:00.830
we have this really
cool thing, which

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:07.070
is Deployment Manager, that you
can easily click on a button

00:28:07.070 --> 00:28:08.320
and then you get a deployment.

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:08.970
Boom.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:09.470
Done.

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:10.610
Easy.

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:12.512
Is there such a
thing for Kubernetes?

00:28:12.512 --> 00:28:13.220
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:15.320
So this is really cool.

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:16.930
If anyone's tracked
along with what

00:28:16.930 --> 00:28:18.190
was Deploy Manager,
and then Helm,

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:19.981
and then backwards and
forwards, everything

00:28:19.981 --> 00:28:22.750
got sort of consolidated
recently, which is really cool.

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:25.250
Everything's now in
Helm for Kubernetes.

00:28:25.250 --> 00:28:26.720
So Helm is this
thing that lets you

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:30.980
deploy common things, like
applications, to Kubernetes.

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:32.950
So some things that
are supported right now

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:35.980
in the stable are Drupal,
Jenkins, WordPress, stuff

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:37.090
like that.

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:39.470
So if you've used
Apt Get, or you've

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:43.290
used Yum, or other
package managers,

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:45.334
such as maybe NPM
or Gem or something

00:28:45.334 --> 00:28:46.250
like that to start a--

00:28:46.250 --> 00:28:47.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Or Yarn.

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:48.640
MARK MANDEL: I've
never used Yarn.

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:50.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It just
was announced today.

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:50.830
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:51.329
Cool.

00:28:51.329 --> 00:28:53.787
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's why.

00:28:53.787 --> 00:28:55.870
MARK MANDEL: Then you can
use that to deploy stuff

00:28:55.870 --> 00:28:56.540
to Kubernetes.

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:58.340
There is a command line client.

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:00.830
What it does is it says
Helm is the command line

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:03.610
client and charts
what the packages are

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:05.760
called within Helm.

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:07.330
And then you can
search through it.

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:08.121
You can initialize.

00:29:08.121 --> 00:29:09.380
You can go Helm Install.

00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:11.490
And that builds
stuff up and puts it

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:13.542
into Kubernetes for you,
which is super cool.

00:29:13.542 --> 00:29:15.000
There's currently
two repositories.

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:16.520
There's the stable repository.

00:29:16.520 --> 00:29:18.380
And then there's the
incubating repository,

00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:20.671
which has some stuff that's
almost there, but not quite

00:29:20.671 --> 00:29:24.080
there yet, which includes
things like HCD and Console

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:26.195
and MongoDB, and a few
other things as well.

00:29:26.195 --> 00:29:28.730
But I think this is super
cool and very exciting

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:30.840
for people who just
want to get stuff up

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:33.390
and running on Kubernetes,
especially if it's common stuff

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:34.800
that everyone's already doing.

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:35.716
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:29:35.716 --> 00:29:38.380
I think that is a very
nice way, especially also,

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:41.430
not only if you are the one
consuming those packages,

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:43.570
but if you have a
cool application

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:45.710
and you want to distribute
it in an easy way.

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:48.540
Anyone can go just
run this command

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:50.400
and get started trying it.

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:52.750
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, absolutely.

00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:53.290
Excellent.

